  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 423 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nRain and thunder have begun to move in across the Minnesota/Iowa \nborder early this morning. Latest SPC mesoanalysis showing low-\nlevel warm advection and frontogenesis occurring right where \nconvection is currently occurring. So far, these storms have \nremained sub-severe with only a few personal weather stations \nrecording some perhaps wind gusts up near 40mph. This storms are\nexpected to continue on a ENE'ly track through much the first \nhalf of today as warm advection continues northward. \n\nToday's forecast remains muddy. Muddy as in regards to the \nquestion of how long this morning \"clutter\" sticks around. Its \nbeen advertised that a second round of thunderstorms is possible\nthis afternoon. The driver of that second round of convection \nwould be a subtle wave that is on the far reaches of an upper-\nlevel troughing pattern over the PacNorthwest. This wave feature\nwill then ride the apex of this ridge then then be pulled into \nanother broad troughing pattern currently situated over southern\nHudson Bay. Upper- forcing will increase across eastern North \nDakota early this afternoon. By then, our AM convection should \nhave cleared east and will allow for redevelopment to occur \nsomewhere over northwestern MN. From here, this is where hi- \nres guidance solutions diverge. HREF/REFS as an example favor a \nbowing MCS to sweep southeast. Where as the HRRR, does also \ncreate a convective complex but does not look as intense and \nkeeps the rain mainly in northern Minnesota. In order for the \nsweeping MCS scenario to come to fruition, our environment would\nrequire to recover quickly and warm up. Also the line would \nneed to develop and then follow the warm/instability gradient \nsouthward. This scenario is what has been driving the SPC's \nsevere weather outlook which has been downgraded from a slight \nrisk to marginal risk for much of central and southern \nMinnesota. \n\nAs we enter Friday into the Labor Day weekend, we will get the \nhot- and- humid forecast on a stick. Once today's precip episode\npasses, ridging builds over the next few days and reaches \nbeyond the Canadian border. This will allow for ample moisture \nand warm air to sit right over MN/WI for the next few days. As \nof now, Friday appears to be the hottest day, as heat indices \nwill climb into the mid to upper 90s as far north as the I-94 \ncorridor. For those within the Twin Cities metro area, it is \npossible that a Heat Advisory may be needed for Friday \nafternoon. As for precip, chances for rain and storms will exist\neach day thanks to additional waves moving along the ridge in \nquasi-zonal flow. Still to far out to determine impacts but at \nleast there is hope for some to get a little drought relief. \nTemperatures will eventually come back to reality by the middle \nof next week.",
